      <span>Brand invisible on Google</span>
    </nav>
    <span class="hero-eyebrow reveal"><img width="918" height="832" src="/images/icon-orange.webp" alt="" /> Leak #2 · Invisibility</span>
    <h1 class="reveal">Your products are excellent. Impossible to find on Google. <em>Here's why.</em></h1>
    <p class="hero-sub reveal">Your shea butter might be the best on the market. Your community knows it. Google doesn't — and when a customer searches for your product without going through Instagram, she finds everything except you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sec" style="padding-top:clamp(24px,4vw,48px)">
  <div class="wrap article reveal">
    <p class="lead">It's almost never a quality problem, and rarely a content problem. Here are the real causes, and the test to identify yours in five minutes.</p>

    <figure>
      <img width="1080" height="720" src="/images/article-google-recherche.webp" alt="Google search on a laptop — every customer's first move before buying" loading="lazy" />
    </figure>

    <h2>8 times out of 10, it's not a content problem — it's a structure problem</h2>
    <p>Intuition says: "write more, start a blog, add descriptions". Sometimes that's true. But on most of the brand sites I audit, the blocker sits further upstream: Google doesn't <em>see</em> the pages, or doesn't understand what they sell.</p>
    <p>The causes that come up most often:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pages that aren't indexed</strong>: Google simply never recorded them. They don't exist in its index, so they don't exist for anyone.</li>
      <li><strong>Internal product names</strong>: your range is called "Royal Glow" — your customer searches "curl defining cream". No bridge between the two.</li>
      <li><strong>Orphan pages</strong>: no navigation leads to them, apart from a link in a story eight months ago. Google judges a page partly by the paths that lead to it.</li>
      <li><strong>A site built as a showroom</strong>: gorgeous for whoever already has the address, mute to a search engine.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The good news: structure problems get fixed faster than content problems. You just need to know which one is yours.</p>

    <h2>The 5-minute test: check where you stand yourself</h2>
    <p>You don't need a single paid tool.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Type <code>site:yourbrand.com</code> into Google.</strong> The number of results ≈ what Google knows about your site. Compare it with your real page count (products + collections + pages). A massive gap = an indexing problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Search "[your brand] + [your hero product]".</strong> If your site isn't first, note who is: that's your hottest leak.</li>
      <li><strong>Search for your hero product <em>like a customer</em></strong> — by what it does ("curl cream for 4C hair"), not by its range name. If you're nowhere within two pages, the naming problem is confirmed.</li>
      <li><strong>Open Search Console</strong> (free, 10 min to set up if you haven't) → "Pages": the number of "not indexed" pages tells you the size of the job.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>This test tells you <em>whether</em> you have a problem and <em>which one</em>. It doesn't tell you how much it costs you or where to start — we're getting there.</p>

    <h2>Name your products the way your customer searches for them</h2>
    <p>The range name is your branding: keep it. But a product page has to carry <em>both</em> languages — yours and search's.</p>
    <p>"Royal Glow" becomes "Royal Glow — Curl Defining Cream for 3C-4C hair". Your identity stays, and the page becomes findable by the thousands of customers who don't know your vocabulary yet. That's exactly the principle of <a href="/en/cultural-seo/">cultural SEO</a>: speaking your community's real language — including in your title tags.</p>
    <p>This naming work happens product by product, crossing your ranges with your market's real queries. It's one of the typical workstreams that come out of the Diagnostic.</p>

    <h2>You've done the test. Now what?</h2>
    <p>If the test reveals a gap, you now know part of your demand is slipping past you — without knowing how much, or which cause to fix first. <a href="/en/lost-organic-revenue-calculator/">Start by estimating the amount with the free calculator</a>.</p>
    <p>To go from the estimate to the real number — your pages, your queries, your prioritised fix plan: <a href="/en/seo-diagnostic/">the AfriRank Diagnostic</a>. €290 (~$310), credited toward your first month if you continue.</p>
  </div>
